WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:26.276
Many of the people in the top 100

00:00:26.276 --> 00:00:29.195
are barely breaking even, 
 sometimes in the red.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.948
If you're not playing at 
Grand Slams consistently

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:34.534
and doing well...
you are struggling.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.624
When I grew up, I always 
watched tennis.

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:44.753
So watching Williams sisters
and everything

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:46.921
my sister and I, we grew up 
playing together

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:49.674
and so I always wanted to be 
like Venus and Serena.

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:53.678
So I come from Poland, which is 
not a huge tennis culture.

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:56.139
I would say my mom 
used to play tennis

00:00:56.139 --> 00:01:00.018
and that's how I started 
when I wanted to...

00:01:00.018 --> 00:01:02.437
become one of the best players 
in the world.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.483
I was the first number one junior 
in 37 years, female.

00:01:06.941 --> 00:01:10.028
And kind of all my dreams 
started coming true.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.655
The perception versus the reality

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:15.825
like tennis does look very glam 
and it looks so polished.

00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:18.912
The highest paid this,
and these big purses

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.413
and these big prize money tournaments...

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.416
you really don't see the struggle.

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:24.751
That was something that...

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.421
I didn't really understand 
until I got to that level.

00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:31.091
And then once I got to 
the top 100...

00:01:31.841 --> 00:01:33.927
then my ranking fell to 400.

00:01:34.302 --> 00:01:37.388
And then that was the time when 
I had to go back

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:39.432
to playing like the really 
low level events.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:42.936
And I didn't know how I was going 
to pay my bills.

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.146
I told my coach like there were times like...

00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:46.147
“Hey, I can’t pay you.”

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:47.649
or  “I can’t pay for training 
this week.”

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:49.234
or “Can you pay for your flight?”

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:50.944
And then, “Yo, I got you.”

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:52.195
When we get to the slam and like

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:53.571
when we get to the bigger tournaments.

00:01:55.573 --> 00:01:57.867
So the structure of tennis

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.078
is pretty unique in the 
sports landscape.

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:02.956
There's a men's tour: the ATP.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.041
There's a women's tour: the WTA.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.627
There's four Grand Slams, 
which are each

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.004
really independently owned 
and operated.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:12.340
There's the International Tennis Federation

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.384
which is like the global governing body

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:15.969
that governs things like the Olympics

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.638
and some of the more global 
aspects of tennis.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.641
And you have this really disjointed structure...

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:24.936
When you add it all up, has left
not only the players behind

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:25.937
and made it something

00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:27.772
that is not anywhere close

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:29.983
to where it should be in relation 
to other sports.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.361
You’d think the total revenue of tennis
could be better. 

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.364
The percentage that the players
are getting, I think that...

00:02:36.906 --> 00:02:38.491
that's also some issue.

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:40.410
The tournaments are sharing

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.538
about 18% of the revenue 
with the players.

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:47.584
In many of those other sports,
hockey, American football, soccer...

00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:50.503
you see a revenue share that is 
much closer to 50%.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.923
As a player, sometimes it is frustrating because

00:02:53.923 --> 00:02:55.425
you know that you're the talent

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:57.177
and you know that you're bringing

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.720
this certain level of entertainment

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.805
to all of these different places 
around the world...

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:03.683
and not being compensated for it.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.686
Tennis is a sport where 
there are no teams.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.897
And so it...

00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:13.735
if anything, that revenue share 
should be higher in tennis...

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:17.071
given the fact that people 
are coming to see

00:03:17.071 --> 00:03:19.407
Player A play against
Player B. 

00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:23.036
Carlos Alcaraz made the most money 
on either the men’s tour

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.829
or the women's tour last year, 
about $10 Million. 

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:27.248
Who is, by the way, 
a generational talent...

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:30.126
who won an incredible amount 
of matches last year.

00:03:31.461 --> 00:03:33.880
And let’s asterisk that for a second 
because that's gross.

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:36.966
That's before he pays his own travel, 
his coaches...

00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:41.512
his physios, all the expenses 
that tennis players bear alone.

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:44.098
That no athlete in a team sport

00:03:44.098 --> 00:03:46.768
or other sports, like F1,
have to bear.

00:03:47.018 --> 00:03:48.811
And if you look at $10 million...

00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:51.731
how that translates in basketball, 
that's roughly

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.817
the 150th highest paid player 
in the NBA.

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:57.070
And then you look at football, 
American football

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.614
that’s 202nd highest salary.

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:01.658
In baseball, it’s a similar story

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:03.117
where it's in the hundreds...

00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:06.788
out of a 6-700 player community 
at the major league level.

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:10.124
That graphic is like tattooed in my head

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:12.043
because it's astonishing to me.

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:30.270
There are a lot more players

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.772
that are struggling than 
the ones that aren't.

00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:36.734
A good friend of mine, Dustin Brown, 
a very, very good player.

00:04:37.235 --> 00:04:39.570
He literally was living out of his car.

00:04:40.154 --> 00:04:42.448
He was stringing rackets

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.368
for the players at the tournament 
to make money.

00:04:45.493 --> 00:04:49.372
And sometimes the amount that 
he was making to string rackets

00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:51.249
was more than he made in his prize money.

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:53.918
And he beat Nadal at Wimbledon.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.878
Like, played one of the most epic matches.

00:04:56.921 --> 00:04:58.423
“Game set and that’s Brown.”

00:04:59.173 --> 00:05:02.260
And the pressure always gets 
spun around and put onto them.

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:04.262
Well, if you just won more, you'd be fine.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:05.471
So what's your problem?

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.765
Well, if you weren't in the two hundreds 
or three hundreds, well, then

00:05:07.765 --> 00:05:09.058
you'd have nothing to complain about.

00:05:09.058 --> 00:05:09.892
As opposed to...

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:11.102
Hey, wait a second.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.854
In all these other global sports...

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:15.690
if I'm in the two hundreds, 
three hundreds...

00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:20.653
I'm actually well entrenched 
as a professional athlete.

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:23.114
Why isn't that the case in tennis?

00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:26.743
It's one of the only sports where 
we're not salary base players.

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:28.536
Your pay is based on your performance.

00:05:28.786 --> 00:05:30.371
The way those other sports 
are structured

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:31.873
the players are employees.

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:34.125
And so when you're an employee, 
you have a minimum salary...

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:39.088
and part of the problem with tennis 
is that the players, despite...

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:41.382
working 11 and a half months a year...

00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:42.842
traveling the globe

00:05:42.842 --> 00:05:44.719
are treated as independent contractors.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:46.929
I guess people who don't 
have endorsements...

00:05:46.971 --> 00:05:49.515
they only make their money 
from tournaments.

00:05:50.058 --> 00:05:52.602
And I don't think that 
that should be a thing.

00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:54.812
I think we should make like 
salary for the year.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.357
I'm fortunate to have, you know...

00:05:57.398 --> 00:05:58.900
a couple of great sponsors.

00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:01.569
I’m wearing a watch while I play. 

00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:04.238
Actually, this watch while I play. 

00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:07.867
Also, I'm playing with a Yonex racket, 
a Yonex outfit

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:11.204
and having a patch on my sleeve 
for Easy Post. 

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.748
I'm sponsored by ASICS 
at the moment.

00:06:14.165 --> 00:06:16.501
They send you a package of clothes

00:06:16.501 --> 00:06:18.711
to wear at different tournaments.

00:06:19.128 --> 00:06:23.424
That's based on your ranking and also 
your profile as a player.

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:25.968
And if you're 200 in the world, maybe...

00:06:26.344 --> 00:06:29.555
you know, maybe there's a local company 
from your country

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.724
that wants to help you, wants to support you.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.893
But if you're outside of top 50...

00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:37.271
you will have a clothing, the other 
you will have a racket deal.

00:06:37.271 --> 00:06:39.774
But it would be touch to have a real source

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:42.068
of income outside of a tennis court.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:46.989
And in my particular situation, 
I don't have any sponsors.

00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:50.368
So, like all of my expenses come out 
of my prize money.

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:54.372
Traveling with a couple of people around the world.

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:56.582
You know, you fly always last minute

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.668
and you sleep in the hotels

00:06:58.668 --> 00:07:00.878
you eat in restaurants.

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:04.924
Those expenses are really, really significant.

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:10.346
Maybe a coach a year is around 
$75,000 without expenses.

00:07:10.346 --> 00:07:13.641
So for hotel, flights

00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:15.268
all that, it could add up.

00:07:16.144 --> 00:07:18.104
There's so much emphasis on the Grand Slams

00:07:18.104 --> 00:07:21.149
because these four times a year, 
only four...

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:24.527
are where you get basically the biggest payout.

00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:26.320
When you're at the lower rankings 

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:27.947
or you're trying to break through.

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:29.740
Like you kind of have to go everywhere

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.118
because you need to get points, 
you need to get money.

00:07:32.493 --> 00:07:34.203
Points are literally everything.

00:07:34.203 --> 00:07:36.706
I mean, that's how our rankings 
are determined.

00:07:36.706 --> 00:07:39.459
And every tournament has 
a certain point value...

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:41.794
and every round has a certain point value.

00:07:42.044 --> 00:07:45.047
So at the Grand Slams is where you can 
make the most points.

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:47.633
And the lower level tournaments like the 250s

00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:48.843
or even like challenger...

00:07:49.093 --> 00:07:51.345
the lower you drop down, 
the less point

00:07:51.345 --> 00:07:52.555
opportunities that you have to make.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.433
So it's just back and forth, back and forth, 
back and forth.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:58.269
Often times the expenses 
outweigh the pay.

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:01.647
The sport hasn't kept pace 
with other sports.

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:05.109
The players and what they generate 
from an income standpoint...

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:06.444
hasn't kept pace.

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:08.070
The protections are non-existent.

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:09.822
There's no safety net. 
There's no minimum.

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:10.990
Because what happens if you get hurt?

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:13.659
What happens if you want to 
take a break?

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:15.453
What happens if you want to 
have a child?

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.663
There's no mechanism for that...

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:21.792
other than putting 100% of that burden 
on the player.

00:08:22.418 --> 00:08:25.004
Fortunately, my parents were able

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:27.423
to help me a lot in my career

00:08:27.423 --> 00:08:29.800
and I'm really thankful to them.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:33.137
If you don't have support, 
it's really challenging.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:34.305
The risk...

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:37.183
is that we can...

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.436
miss out on really unique

00:08:40.436 --> 00:08:43.272
and amazing talents and people 
who are very, very good...

00:08:44.106 --> 00:08:45.316
because they can't afford to play.

00:08:45.942 --> 00:08:47.443
Players like Ons Jabeur...

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.571
she didn't have those opportunities 
growing up in Tunisia.

00:08:51.072 --> 00:08:52.281
Frances Tiafoe.

00:08:52.323 --> 00:08:53.658
I mean, those are players who made it...

00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:55.368
despite those challenges, 
made it.

00:08:55.493 --> 00:08:57.703
What about all the other players that didn't?

00:08:58.246 --> 00:09:00.831
I'm trying to identify and really get clear on like...

00:09:01.457 --> 00:09:03.167
how can I make the biggest difference?

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:05.920
I feel like if I don't do that or if I don't try, like...

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:07.755
I'm wasting...

00:09:07.755 --> 00:09:09.507
all the things that I've been through

00:09:09.507 --> 00:09:10.883
and the lessons that I learn.

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:13.344
Like I want to pass that on 
to as many people as I can.

00:09:13.344 --> 00:09:16.097
And if I can shape a next
generational talent...

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:17.515
I will love that.

